# Source-grounded critique: biomedical image review workspace

**Stage:** I-05-CONTROL-ARTIFACT-RECOVERY-R001-critique-a001  
**Review date:** 2026-10-05  
**Scope:** critique of the complete frozen research proposal and its supporting catalogs. This review is not an application test or final proposal.

## Overall assessment

Keep the proposal’s main direction: a narrow reader boundary; napari as a viewing/editing component rather than the project authority; project-owned coordinates, annotations, identity and provenance; SQLite for small transactional state; large source and derived arrays outside the database; read-only sources; closed-bundle exchange; explicit refusal or user correction for ambiguous inputs; and sidecars when a standard export cannot express the complete project contract. These choices fit the brief and are stated as proposals rather than demonstrated application behavior.

The strongest research finding is the napari split-RGB failure followed through patch, regression test and the v0.6.5 tag. The proposal correctly limits its applicability. Its numerical coordinate witness W1 is also correctly scoped to isolated arithmetic. W2–W6 are acceptance plans, all **proposed / UNEXECUTED**; nothing in the evidence establishes the 3 GB memory target, end-to-end coordinate fidelity, crash recovery, export round-trips, or accessibility.

Resolve R1–R4 before treating physical coordinates, transformed exports, identity verification, or recovery as an implementation contract. R5–R6 refine the support and precedent claims without invalidating the architecture.

## Findings and required corrections

### R1 — Separate OME pixel spacing from stage positions and per-plane timing

**External fact.** OME-TIFF 6.2.2 says `DimensionOrder` gives plane rasterization order and `TiffData` can assign IFDs to Z/T/C positions explicitly; a reader must not equate page number with application axes. (Frozen source S2, “DimensionOrder” and “The TiffData element”; capture SHA-256 `fcc4fb9dbcee7ad66a73a10735eb2aec335805c448a239f375ade9d0702e2584`.) The OME 2016-06 schema distinguishes `Pixels/@PhysicalSizeX/Y/Z`—optional pixel sizes with optional unit attributes whose schema default is µm—from `Plane/@PositionX/Y/Z`, which are microscope stage positions for an indexed channel/Z/time plane and have a schema default unit of “reference frame.” It also distinguishes global `Pixels/@TimeIncrement` from optional per-plane `Plane/@DeltaT`. (Pinned schema URL and exact capture in source S11 below.) These fields do not, by themselves, define one universal oriented affine from array indices to a laboratory frame.

**Correction.** Keep the proposal’s float64 spatial affine and explicit null/override path, but specify the adapter rule: preserve raw XML and parser-effective values separately; show when µm or seconds came from an OME schema default rather than a literal unit attribute; never treat a `Plane.Position*` value as the affine origin without a documented interpretation; and do not collapse varying per-plane stage positions or `DeltaT` into uniform spatial/time scale. Where the source cannot establish one mapping, expose the raw fields and keep physical coordinates unavailable until a user records a mapping, or use a separately specified per-plane/per-time model. Also report XML/TIFF structural conflicts and which source value the reader used. Keep the source unchanged.

**Affected dependencies:** proposal §§ “Supported input boundary,” “Coordinate, image, and annotation contract,” and “Minimum workflow and exports”; W4/W5. Add fixtures for missing unit attributes, nonzero and varying plane positions, irregular `DeltaT`, global `TimeIncrement`, and conflicting OME-XML/TIFF dimensions. This is a clarification to the proposed contract, not evidence that the isolated W1 calculation is wrong.

### R2 — Bind annotation geometry to both its source grid and any derived grid

**Engineering inference.** The proposal gives each annotation a source asset ID/content identity and gives geometric transforms their own derived-data record. It does not yet state which geometry remains canonical when an image is cropped, resampled, rotated or sheared, or how an annotation is linked to the resulting derived grid. This is the specific boundary behind the researchers’ disagreement about whether an exported annotation still refers to the same image.

**Correction.** Define each annotation’s anchor as an immutable asset identity plus grid/axis mapping and coordinate frame. Keep that source geometry unchanged. When an operation creates a new grid, record an explicit source-grid-to-derived-grid mapping and operation ID; if a mapped annotation copy is made, give it a distinct identity and provenance rather than silently moving the original. Annotation tables, masks, overlays and derived-image manifests should identify both the source asset digest and (when relevant) the derived asset digest, grid, transform/operation chain, annotation IDs, time/slice/channel selection, and whether coordinates are source-bound or derived-grid coordinates. If an exporter cannot encode the mapping, mark the export incomplete/lossy or require explicit resampling as a new derived operation.

**Affected dependencies:** proposal §§ 58–64 and 90–95; W5 and W6. Extend the proposed check with source-bound geometry over a crop, anisotropic resampling, reflection and rotation/shear, then read back the annotation table, overlay and derived-image manifest. Preserve the current rule that view transforms and intensity display changes do not mutate source pixels.

### R3 — Make source identity stable across hashing, reading and export

**Engineering inference.** A SHA-256 value identifies the bytes actually hashed. A read-only application handle prevents writes through that handle, but cannot prevent another process from changing a linked file or Zarr tree while it is being hashed or read. A digest can therefore describe a different or mixed state from the data the user saw unless the import/session contract handles concurrent mutation. The proposed “sorted normalized paths + size + object hash + canonical manifest” is directionally sound, but “normalized” and tree membership are not yet defined.

**Correction.** State when annotation is allowed while identity is `pending`; how the application establishes that a full-file or tree digest corresponds to the stable data being decoded; what happens if data changes during a session; and when identity must be rechecked before a verified export or relocation. Use a quiescent source, a verified immutable portable copy/snapshot, or a documented change-detection policy with a conservative unavailable/changed state. Do not treat filename, size, timestamps, or a hash that raced with mutation as content proof. For Zarr v2 trees, version the manifest and define path encoding/ordering, object types and bytes included, metadata inclusion, symlink handling, duplicate/case-collision behavior, and incomplete-tree handling. Keep the digest pending or invalid if the tree changes during enumeration or hashing.

**Affected dependencies:** proposal §§ 72–80 and workflow step 4; W3/W4; leads L2–L4. Add tests that mutate/replace a source during hashing, after a successful hash, and during a multi-object Zarr scan; test missing/added objects and a same-name, changed-content relocation. This preserves the useful content-addressed relink design while making its guarantee precise.

### R4 — Specify durability across artifact publication and the SQLite commit

**External fact.** SQLite’s atomic-commit article describes rollback-journal behavior and explicitly depends on filesystem/VFS locking, flush and file-operation assumptions; it does not certify network shares. (Frozen S10; captured page SHA-256 `5a5be7b660217c9408c8a81eaf2faa2d08832ed3790f895a0a713916ad856ea1`.) The proposal is right to keep live editing local and exchange closed bundles only after testing the actual storage.

**Correction.** Make the publication order and recovery cases explicit: write and verify staged artifact data; flush artifact files; publish on the same filesystem; flush the relevant directory metadata where the platform supports it; only then durably commit the database pointer. A rename plus SQLite transaction is not a single cross-resource transaction. The described orphan-artifact case is recoverable, but the reverse crash ordering can leave a committed pointer whose renamed artifact was not durably published. Define startup handling for missing, partial and unreferenced artifacts, and make the selected journal and synchronous settings explicit and versioned. Qualify rename, lock, flush and copy behavior on each actual shared-storage type before relying on it; do not generalize local-disk results to a share.

**Affected dependencies:** proposal §§ 70–80; W3 and leads L4. Add fault injection before/after each artifact flush, rename, directory flush and DB commit, then test reopen and closed-bundle copy/reopen on each target filesystem. The current local-first, one-writer design remains appropriate.

### R5 — Tighten the format claim to the OME-NGFF 0.4 rules

**External fact.** NGFF 0.4 specifies Zarr v2; multiscale images have 2–5 dimensions, with 2 or 3 space axes and at most one time axis plus one channel or custom/null axis. Axis metadata must agree with array dimension order; time precedes channel/custom, which precedes space. Dataset transforms are scale plus optional translation, applied in order, with translation after scale; this vocabulary cannot express a general rotation/shear affine. Label images use integer values and the image-label source link is optional. (Frozen S1 §§ 2, 3.1, 3.3–3.7; exact capture SHA-256 `ca4780a33f9561cfd2d983651fdcc745dce0d6089f5cf775744a54e8394a5269`.)

**Correction.** The proposed subset and sidecar/resampling options are sound, but state that ordinary NGFF acceptance validates the 0.4 axis count/order, transform lengths/order, multiscale shapes and label-grid relationship. If a user overrides malformed or unsupported axes, label the import as an explicit project interpretation/correction, not as unmodified conformant NGFF metadata. Distinguish an optional image-label `source.image` path from a guaranteed relationship. The proposal’s broad “axis order permutations” validation should say which cases are valid source metadata and which are explicit correction/refusal cases.

**Affected dependencies:** proposal §§ 9, 31–38, 54–64, 90–95; W4/W5; leads L2/L5/L6. Keep rotation/shear in the project sidecar or make a recorded resampled derivative; standard NGFF-only metadata cannot carry that full affine.

### R6 — Preserve the napari repair chain, narrow its test claim, and keep performance issue #9515 as a lead

**Verified repair history.** Issue #7085 reports that “Split RGB” discarded a nontrivial layer transform on napari `0.4.16.dev1413+ga4fa1fa0` (issue API capture SHA-256 `23d800f8f4421c35ff8bd159a310011a53a60e409f5ffff422bde9b2708d0ad3`). PR #8256 explains that `split_rgb` used `stack_to_images`, which dropped affine/rotate/shear, and changes the path to `split_channels`; patch capture SHA-256 `8819f356306877e0db71cf13884a9bee57ae476cbd62ed6a1834cf83912e47df`. The patch’s three code-bearing commits are `2b51110e65af469863697797921ff12aa668d301`, `3f994d36130e5d673ce059a1a0d66fac9cca03b0`, and `9f262998cbe464e0ec826eae691e487af752658c`; the PR also contains two merge commits. GitHub’s PR record identifies merge SHA `afd1bb88f296726fe98bc0a94119a51bba6870c4`, merge on 2025-09-26, and milestone 0.6.5 (PR API capture SHA-256 `d5014177d9db6ff47e5e92855e7d03572a60c6c01eb1efa9ad867cb189978e67`). The v0.6.5 source and test are captured separately as S4/S5 and show the fix in the selected tag.

**Narrowing.** The tagged test covers no transform, scale, translation, and `Affine(translate=[0, 4])`. That final case is an affine wrapper around translation, not a general affine matrix. The source change handles `rotate` and `shear` in the split path, but the regression test does not assert rotation/shear or project persistence/export. Retain the proposal’s claim as evidence of a real view-operation metadata bug and a tested narrow fix; do not cite it as general affine or whole-application fidelity evidence. Extend W5 with a non-diagonal affine, rotation/shear/reflection, split/view operations, save/reopen and export.

**Performance report limits.** Napari issue #9515 remains an open report, not a repair chain. It describes slow/over-fetching behavior for particular multidimensional multiscale local Zarr data in 2D display; its report/API capture SHA-256 is `54172f653d6c6fdc7e96cefd7a408d3ec8787354b191c1c4709a1e0c035487e6`, and its comments capture is `a20fb19679dc634101bb2cf91858245c3e5b39fae80aafbec0f13bd55480e67d`. There is a version discrepancy in the issue: its inline reproduction requirements say `napari[pyqt6]==0.9.1` and `zarr>=3`, while the attached environment lists `napari 0.6.1rc1.dev1` and Dask 2026.8.0. Neither report configuration is the proposal’s napari 0.6.5 / Zarr 2.18.7 candidate. Keep the report as a reason to measure real read amplification and cancellation; do not imply it demonstrates a defect in the proposed pin. W2’s exact-stack local fixtures, source bytes, RSS, cache/in-flight buffers, latency and cancellation measurements are the right next evidence and remain UNEXECUTED.

## Precedents, pins and evidence quality

The proposal has distinct mechanisms worth preserving. Napari v0.6.5 supplies multidimensional display/layer behavior, lazy wrapping of direct Zarr arrays in Dask in `slice_from_axis`, and a concrete transform-preservation lesson in `split_rgb` (S4/S5). Tifffile 2025.5.10 documents an `aszarr=True` read path, regional access to TIFF tiles through Zarr v2, and a Dask adapter example; it also states Zarr 3 incompatibility and a multi-file pyramidal OME-TIFF limitation (S3). These are independent viewer and reader precedents, not evidence of one integrated application. SQLite’s atomic-commit article supplies a separate storage mechanism under its stated assumptions (S10). Make this independence explicit in the next proposal and label the evidence accurately: napari has tagged implementation plus regression test; tifffile’s frozen evidence is release documentation/examples, not an executed reader test; SQLite’s source is an unversioned technical article, not a lab-filesystem test.

The named component pins are plausible, not a resolved environment. Napari v0.6.5 declares Python `>=3.10`, Dask arrays and tifffile dependencies, and Zarr as optional; its project file does not lock Qt, codecs, or the full dependency graph (S8, exact capture SHA-256 `b95458e66fbdaee45a26d876e6411735e31f4113753aa4f1d4c6b56bf6604354`). The tifffile release documentation requires Zarr 2 for this path and the pinned 2.18.7 matches it, but neither source proves cross-platform integration or the 3 GB budget. Keep lead L1 and the proposed lock/build qualification.

## Supported claims to carry forward

- OME-TIFF IFD order must be interpreted through `TiffData` and `DimensionOrder`; a narrow, one-series qualification boundary is defensible (S2/S3).
- NGFF 0.4 supplies axes, chunked arrays, scale/translation transforms and integer image-label groups, but does not replace the application’s full coordinate/provenance contract (S1).
- Display mapping can remain view state; a geometric resample should create a recorded derived object. The architecture supports both without rewriting the original.
- Local-only single-writer editing and closed-bundle exchange are prudent product choices while shared-storage semantics remain unqualified (S10; L4).
- Streaming identity, reference-versus-portable storage, explicit relink verification, refusal/correction choices, temporary exports, recovery checks, readable errors, styles, keyboard navigation, undo/redo and accessibility are all appropriate obligations. Their implementation is still proposed.
- The 3 GB target, responsiveness numbers and all W2–W6 behavior are targets/check procedures, not findings. W1 verifies only its chosen arithmetic example and does not test a parser, viewer, project, filesystem or exporter.
- Optional NGFF label export and separately qualified Bio-Formats/vendor conversion remain useful leads; neither is an MVP compatibility promise. Keep collaboration merge, cloud, GPU, segmentation and registration optional as proposed.

